          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-744e2a3bb39d2399\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":2,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Natasha's repeated “I love you” is principally an imagined avowal of her enduring love for the dead Prince Andrei. It also gains moderate undertaking force from the imagined exchange in which she declares willingness to bind herself to him and share his suffering. The repetition and convulsive physical effort mark extreme grief and intensity, but the text frames the words as tender things she wishes she had deliberately said, not as a reflex-like verbal discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The narration may imply that Natasha voices the words aloud while immersed in the imagined scene. Performative force is present contextually but is less securely encoded by the highlighted words themselves.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"imagined\",\"description\":\"Natasha addresses Prince Andrei within a counterfactual reconstruction of their final conversation, apparently voicing the imagined declaration while physically alone after his death.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Grieving after Prince Andrei's death, Natasha mentally repairs a past answer that she believes he misunderstood. Her declaration expresses what she wishes she had made known: life without him is empty, and sharing even his continual suffering would have been happiness. The utterance is therefore retrospective and impossible to communicate to its intended recipient, combining avowal with a counterfactual acceptance of lasting relational burden. Her repeated wording, tears, clasped hands, and clenched teeth convey agonized intensity, but do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«И в воображении своем она говорила ему еще другие нежные, любовные речи, которые она могла бы сказать тогда» (“In her imagination she said to him still other tender, loving words that she could have said then”).\",\"supports\":\"Identifies the declaration as an imagined, counterfactual avowal consciously supplying words left unsaid in the past.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«Я люблю тебя... тебя... люблю, люблю...»\",\"supports\":\"Directly presents Natasha's loving emotional state as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":1.0},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"«без вас нет ничего в жизни, и страдать с вами для меня лучшее счастие» (“without you there is nothing in life for me, and to suffer with you is the greatest happiness for me”).\",\"supports\":\"Supports the sincerity and depth of the avowal and gives some P support by framing love as willingness to share enduring suffering.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Prince Andrei's imagined prompt concerns «связать себя навеки с страдающим человеком» (“to bind oneself forever to a suffering person”), which Natasha answers by accepting suffering with him.\",\"supports\":\"Places the love declaration within an exchange about permanent relational commitment, supporting moderate rather than merely affectionate P force.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"She speaks while «судорожно сжимая руки, стискивая зубы с ожесточенным усилием» (“convulsively clasping her hands, clenching her teeth with fierce effort”).\",\"supports\":\"Shows intense, effortful grief and delivery, but supplies no positive evidence that the words themselves are a reflex-like discharge rather than an emotionally intense imagined avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal, while P captures its contextually supported acceptance of lasting relational burden. Delivery and grief can be represented contextually without E, and no important aspect of the core force remains outside T/P/E.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The analytical translation was used only as an aid to reading the supplied Russian source; interpretive evidence is grounded in the local source text.\"}"
        }
